{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01VfwyAuLtvH7tPP33LKX61P","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — contamcal-irt-calibration\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- **Primary: EMNLP 2026 ARR May 25** (D006: reinstated per user directive, 12 days)\n5\t- Fallback: AAAI 2027 (Jul-Aug 2026) / ICLR 2027 (Sep 2026)\n6\t- Track: Machine Learning for NLP / Resources and Evaluation\n7\t- Aligns with EMNLP 2026 special theme \"New Missions for NLP Research\" (explicitly invites contamination/data governance work)\n8\t\n9\t## Core Insight (D039 pivot + D108 domain heterogeneity)\n10\tBenchmark contamination effects are far more complex than \"scores go up.\" Through controlled SFT injection and contamination-augmented IRT analysis, we reveal: effects exhibit domain-level heterogeneity within benchmarks (MMLU: Simpson's paradox — aggregate γ≈0 masks knowledge↑ vs reasoning↓ pattern), are benchmark-dependent across benchmarks (ARC: forgetting vs GSM8K: inflation), scale-dependent (catastrophic at 0.5B, negligible at 7B), and detection signals exhibit fundamental failure modes (Min-K++ directional reversal). ContamCal uses IRT with per-benchmark γ_k to systematically characterize these behaviors and establish identifiability boundaries. Calibration is possible conditionally — when model complexity matches benchmark structure (2PL for open-ended, 4PL for MCQ) and signal quality is sufficient (recovery validated down to AUC=0.55, lowest tested, at J≥50) — but the primary contribution is the characterization framework itself. Grounded in educational measurement's item compromise IRT (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) and explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004).\n11\t\n12\t## Core Claims (4 条 — D039 reorder: characterization first, calibration demoted)\n13\t\n14\t1. **Characterization Claim (PRIMARY — D108: domain heterogeneity evidence)**: Contamination effects exhibit domain-level heterogeneity within benchmarks, are benchmark-dependent across benchmarks, and scale-dependent. Within MMLU, stratification by cognitive domain (57 subjects → 8 domains) reveals Simpson's paradox: aggregate γ≈0 masks domain-level heterogeneity (5 domains significant, γ range: -0.55 to +1.34). Pattern: knowledge-intensive domains (history, law) show positive γ; reasoning-intensive domains (STEM, formal logic) show negative γ. Claim is based on **pattern/ranking consistency**, not absolute γ values (PPP=0 in 4/5 domains, see strategy below). Pending N=24 rerun confirmation before upgrading to \"domain-dependent characterization.\" Across benchmarks: ARC forgetting (γ < 0, CI excludes zero, 3 independent evidence chains) vs GSM8K inflation (γ > 0). Effects are scale-modulated: catastrophic at 0.5B (-17pp ARC), mild at 3B, negligible at 7B. This challenges the dominant \"contamination = uniform inflation\" narrative. Cross-family consistency with Llama-3.2-1B suggests contamination susceptibility is a benchmark × domain property, not a model family artifact.\n15\t   **PPP presentation strategy (Reviewer warning)**: Domain-level PPP=0 reflects known sensitivity of posterior predictive checks in large-N IRT (Levy & Svetina 2011; Sinharay 2006). Paper claims based on **pattern/ranking** (knowledge > reasoning domains) and **heterogeneity** (Simpson's paradox), NOT absolute γ values. Will rerun with N=24 (adding 3B) — if γ attenuates, interpret as scale-dependent contamination sensitivity (valuable finding, narrative adjustment needed). Consider limited-information fit indices (M2*, RMSEA2) as robustness check.\n16\t\n17\t2. **Signal Failure Claim (CO-PRIMARY, headline finding per D039 dream 三档一致)**: Min-K++ exhibits directional reversal at small model scales (Cohen's d = -1.2 to -2.7 at 0.5B d100) — actively misleading, not merely ineffective. S1 embedding similarity is the only reliable signal (AUC>0.95). Multi-signal RRF degrades to S1-only in practice — paper is transparent about this (D039: all 3 dream tiers require honesty here). Training-free RRF remains the principled aggregation design (meta-circularity argument). Must distinguish item-level detection AUC from model-level Cohen's d.\n18\t   **C2 contingency**: \"Scale-dependent\" unverified beyond 0.5B. If 1.5B/3B S2/S4 remain ineffective → downgrade to \"method-specific signal effectiveness.\" Min-K++ reversal remains novel regardless.\n19\t\n20\t3. **Identifiability Claim**: Fisher Information analysis + simulation sweep (20 conditions, 200 runs). Recovery succeeds down to AUC=0.55 (lowest tested) at N≥50; the lower bound remains open. LLM evaluation inherently operates below N≥50 (typically N=8-30), creating a structural domain-method mismatch — this is the real \"impossibility,\" not merely \"IRT needs more data.\" CI width ∝ N^{-0.48} (simulation). Coverage collapse at N<30 confirmed empirically (D036). D039 dream worst-tier caution: frame as domain mismatch insight, not as surprising impossibility.\n21\t\n22\t4. **Conditional Calibration Claim (DEMOTED from old Claim 1 per D039)**: When IRT model complexity matches benchmark structure (2PL for open-ended, 4PL for MCQ) AND signal quality sufficient (validated down to AUC=0.55 at J≥50), calibration produces usable point estimates (ARC MAE=0.035). Coverage remains below nominal (20-69% at 95%) — present calibrated CIs as directional indicators, not precise credible intervals. Calibration gating criterion (D039 dream recommendation): γ significant AND PPP ∈ [0.1, 0.9] AND calibrated MAE < raw MAE. GSM8K overcorrection (MAE 11× worse than raw) is an honest negative result demonstrating the gating criterion's necessity.\n23\t\n24\t## Contributions (Hierarchical — D039 rebalanced per Dream 2nd round)\n25\t\n26\t### Core (characterization-led; calibration conditional)\n27\t1. **Domain-dependent contamination characterization** via IRT γ_k: bidirectional effects at domain level (Simpson's paradox within MMLU), scale modulation, three-regime taxonomy (forgetting / selective memorization / near-immunity). γ heterogeneity driven by cognitive task type (knowledge vs reasoning domains)\n28\t2. **Signal failure characterization**: Min-K++ directional reversal (Cohen's d = -1.2 to -2.7) — paper's most citation-worthy empirical finding (D025 + D039 dream 三档一致). Systematic signal × scale × dosage AUC mapping. Honest reporting that RRF = S1-only in practice\n29\t3. **Fisher Information identifiability analysis**: structural vs practical thresholds with domain-method mismatch insight. Strongest theoretical contribution (D025 + D039 一致)\n30\t\n31\t### Supporting\n32\t4. ContamCal framework: two-stage pipeline (S1 provenance → contamination-augmented IRT with per-benchmark γ_k). Conditional calibration with gating criterion. ME model as ablation\n33\t5. Dual-track validation + 7 baselines + 6 metrics (incl. SBC). Controlled SFT injection (internal) + ecological RL (external)\n34\t\n35\t## Story Spine\n36\t- **Problem**: Benchmark scores are increasingly unreliable due to training data contamination. Rephrased test items inflate MMLU accuracy by 8-15 points while evading n-gram decontamination. RL post-training may further complicate detection (under investigation, exp-009). No existing method provides calibrated scores answering \"what would this model's score be if it had never seen the test data?\"\n37\t- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Detection methods (Min-K%++, ConStat, Self-Critique, KDS) flag contamination but don't calibrate scores. IRT methods (PSN-IRT, Growing Pains, ATLAS, Lalor et al. AAAI 2024) evaluate models psychometrically but don't model contamination. DCR provides adjusted accuracy but lacks uncertainty bounds and RL handling. HiBayES provides Bayesian uncertainty but ignores contamination. Emperor's New Clothes (ICML 2025) demonstrates that all 20 mitigation strategies fail — motivating post-hoc calibration. Dynamic benchmarks (LiveBench) offer an alternative but require continuous curation.\n38\t- **Our framing (D039 pivot)**: ContamCal is a diagnostic framework that uses IRT to systematically characterize how contamination affects benchmark scores. Rather than claiming to \"fix\" contaminated scores, we reveal the structure of contamination effects — they are bidirectional, scale-dependent, and benchmark-specific — and establish the conditions under which statistical calibration is and isn't feasible. Grounded in educational measurement's item compromise IRT (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) and explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004), with 3 structural adaptations: (a) independent examinees → single model with joint item distribution, (b) binary leakage → continuous exposure scores, (c) stable ability → regime-conditional ability.\n39\t- **Headline result template (D039 — characterization-led)**: \"Contamination effects are benchmark-dependent: IRT γ captures forgetting on ARC (γ={X}, CI excludes zero) and inflation on GSM8K (γ={Y}), with scale modulation from catastrophic (-{Z}pp at 0.5B) to negligible at 7B. Detection signal Min-K++ actively reverses direction at small scales (Cohen's d = {W}), making single-signal contamination detection unreliable. Fisher Information analysis establishes that reliable γ recovery requires N≥50 models; recovery succeeded down to AUC=0.55 (lowest tested), with the lower bound remaining open — conditions rarely met in current LLM evaluation.\"\n40\t\n41\t## Venue Fit Risks\n42\t- **EMNLP (primary)**: Best venue fit — special theme explicitly invites contamination/data governance. Reviewers deeply familiar with NLP contamination literature (advantage: no need to motivate the problem heavily). Risk: reviewers may be less familiar with IRT/psychometric methods (mitigation: add intuitive explanations, visual pipeline overview, and connection to familiar NLP concepts like calibration/confidence estimation). Risk: may expect broader model coverage beyond Qwen2.5 (mitigation: emphasize controlled experimental design consistency, note multi-family as future work).\n43\t- **AAAI/ICLR (fallback)**: Strong fit for Bayesian/probabilistic methods. Reviewers may need more contamination context but appreciate methodological rigor.\n44\t- **Novelty positioning**: Core method is cross-disciplinary synthesis (detection signals + explanatory IRT + measurement error). NOT positioned as \"new algorithm\" — positioned as (a) signal effectiveness characterization as empirically novel finding, (b) identifiability bounds with theoretical grounding, (c) first framework bridging detection → calibration with proper UQ. RRF is a principled design choice (meta-circularity argument), not claimed as methodological contribution.\n45\t- **RL limitation**: RL models get wide CIs. This is honest — frame as \"calibration is only as good as its inputs.\" Expected regret quantification uses held-out clean benchmark as ground truth (NOT calibrated scores, to avoid circularity).\n46\t\n47\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Defenses\n48\t\n49\t| # | Concern | Defense | Experiment |\n50\t|---|---------|---------|------------|\n51\t| 1 | θ and γ identifiable? | Fisher Information analysis + 20-condition simulation sweep (200 runs) + MVP validation | exp-004 |\n52\t| 2 | Why not learned aggregation? | Meta-circularity: learned aggregator's training data may itself be contaminated; RRF is design choice, not contribution | exp-003 |\n53\t| 3 | SFT injection ≠ real contamination | Dual-track: controlled SFT (internal) + ecological RL (external) + near-zero γ check (specificity). SFT is \"best-available proxy\", acknowledged as limitation | exp-006, exp-009 |\n54\t| 4 | Scale limited to 3B | 7B controlled + 72B observational | exp-007, exp-012 |\n55\t| 5 | Why 4PL not 2PL? | MVP: 2PL γ=0.16 vs 4PL γ=0.34 at N=100; c/d necessary for realistic item modeling | exp-006 |\n56\t| 6 | Too few models (N≈15)? | D025 multi-seed strategy: 3 scales × 4+ dosages × 2-3 seeds = 24-36 models pooled. Target N≥30 minimum (N≥50 ideal). Simulation sweep (exp-004) establishes identifiability at actual N. Growing Pains pre-estimation as backup at very low N | exp-004, exp-001 |\n57\t| 7 | Noisy exposure scores | Measurement error model (exposure as noisy observation of latent τ) propagates uncertainty into posteriors automatically | exp-005 |\n58\t| 8 | No baselines | 7 baselines including DCR (EMNLP 2025) + bootstrap CIs on all comparisons | exp-006 |\n59\t| 9 | Single global γ misspecified? | Per-benchmark γ_k is primary (natural in per-benchmark fitting); joint model with shared global γ is ablation. Report both to show contamination effect heterogeneity across benchmarks | exp-006 |\n60\t| 10 | Local independence violated? | Discussed explicitly; single model responds to all items → item responses correlated through shared parameters. Empirically test via posterior predictive checks on item-level residual correlations. This is a known IRT limitation when N_examinees is small. | exp-006 |\n61\t| 11 | Single model family? | Acknowledged as limitation. Qwen2.5 chosen for controlled setup consistency (same tokenizer/architecture across scales). Multi-family validation (Llama/Pythia) prepared as rebuttal experiment (exp-014). | exp-014 (rebuttal) |\n62\t| 12 | RL regret circular? | Ground truth for regret = held-out clean benchmark scores, NOT calibrated scores. Explicitly stated | exp-009 |\n63\t| 13 | S2/S4 fail at 0.5B — RRF is just S1? | Scale-dependent: honestly report single-signal regime at 0.5B. Verify S2/S4 at 1.5B/3B. RRF value = principled aggregation when signals ARE available + graceful degradation when not. | exp-008, exp-002 |\n64\t| 14 | d100 AUC=0.5 as signal failure? | Labeling artifact: d100 contaminates ALL items → labels lose discriminative power. Must NOT be interpreted as signal degradation. Present only d005/d025 for signal evaluation. | exp-008 |\n65\t| 15 | Novelty ceiling — standard IRT synthesis? (D025, all 3 Dream tiers) | Positioned as cross-disciplinary synthesis, NOT new algorithm. Emergent value: (a) per-benchmark γ_k heterogeneity as benchmark property, (b) Min-K++ reversal as cautionary finding, (c) Fisher Info identifiability boundaries. If 4PL vs 2PL gap small → acknowledge honestly but argue c/d params needed for realistic MC modeling. Report oracle-γ ablation to decompose MAE. | exp-006 |\n66\t| 16 | Per-benchmark γ_k via separate fits, not hierarchical? (D025, [M][W]) | Per-benchmark separate fits = primary (natural, avoids cross-benchmark correlation). Hierarchical γ_k ~ Normal(μ_γ, σ_γ) as ablation to formally test heterogeneity and share info across benchmarks. Both reported. | exp-006 |\n67\t| 17 | Coverage gap — 85% vs 95% nominal? (D025, all 3 tiers) | SBC diagnostic to identify source. Possible causes: local independence violation, insufficient N, model misspecification. Report SBC alongside posterior predictive checks. If gap is from local independence → discuss as known IRT limitation with references. | exp-004, exp-006 |\n68\t| 18 | Why not just S1 alone? (D025, [M][W]) | S1-only is a baseline (#2 single-signal 2PL+γ). If RRF ≤ S1-only → present as \"S1+IRT calibration with extensible multi-signal architecture for future signals.\" Meta-circularity argument for training-free aggregation still holds. | exp-003 |\n69\t| 19 | Coverage collapse 20-33% even with oracle? (D036) | Far worse than #17 anticipated. PPP extremes (ARC=1.0, GSM8K=0.0) = systematic misspecification. Path: (a) increase N via multi-seed pooling, (b) check exp-004 sweep coverage at matching N, (c) if additive γ insufficient → item-level heterogeneity or hierarchical structure. Fallback: lead with MAE+ranking, present coverage honestly. | exp-006, exp-004 |\n70\t| 20 | GSM8K overcorrection — calibration 11× worse than raw (D039, all 3 dream tiers) | Honest negative result. Implement calibration gating: γ significant AND PPP ∈ [0.1,0.9] AND calibrated MAE < raw MAE. Report raw scores when gating fails. Reframe: ContamCal identifies when NOT to calibrate. | exp-006 |\n71\t| 21 | Linear regression nearly as good as IRT (D039, all 3 tiers) | B4 MAE=0.040 vs ContamCal MAE=0.035 on ARC. IRT's value is NOT better MAE — it's interpretable per-item parameters, benchmark-dependent γ_k characterization, and theoretical grounding. Bootstrap CI on MAE gap to show significance (or acknowledge non-significance). | exp-006 |\n72\t| 22 | SFT capacity artifact ≠ contamination (D039, all 3 tiers) | 0.5B ARC forgetting (-17pp) may be capacity displacement, not contamination-specific. Need: (a) SFT-on-random-data control at 0.5B to isolate capacity vs contamination, (b) focus on 1.5B/3B where capacity effects are minimal (<2pp). Acknowledge 0.5B forgetting as combined SFT+contamination effect. | exp-001 |\n73\t| 23 | N=8/16 contradicts paper's own N≥50 recommendation (D039, all 3 tiers) | Internal contradiction. Path: (a) add N=8 condition to simulation sweep for calibrated expectations, (b) increase N via multi-seed (target N≥30), (c) if N<30 → explicitly position as preliminary with caveats. SBC diagnostic at actual N to decompose coverage gap. | exp-004, exp-006 |\n74\t\n75\t## Evaluation Protocol (6 Metrics — D025 added SBC)\n76\t1. **Calibration error**: MAE between calibrated score and ground-truth clean accuracy, with bootstrap 95% CIs\n77\t2. **Interval coverage**: Fraction of true values within 50%/80%/95% credible intervals\n78\t3. **Interval sharpness**: Average CI width (tighter is better at fixed coverage)\n79\t4. **Ranking preservation**: Kendall's τ between calibrated and ground-truth model rankings\n80\t5. **Dose-response monotonicity**: γ effect increases monotonically with contamination dosage\n81\t6. **SBC diagnostic** (D025): Simulation-Based Calibration (Talts et al. 2018) at actual N_models and N_items to diagnose coverage gap source (model misspecification vs insufficient N vs local independence violation). CPU-only, parallelize with GPU experiments\n82\t\n83\t## Benchmarks (2 main + 2 contrasts)\n84\t1. **MMLU** — knowledge-intensive multiple-choice (14K items, subsampled 1000 for NUTS) — PRIMARY\n85\t2. **ARC-Challenge** — science reasoning multiple-choice (~1.2K items) — PRIMARY. ⚠️ D036: gamma consistently negative (forgetting, not inflation). 3 independent evidence chains: s42 raw (-17.5pp), s123 raw (-10.2pp), oracle IRT (γ=-2.83). Scale-dependent: 3B d100 ARC=84% (mild forgetting) vs 0.5B d100 ARC=29-37% (catastrophic). Paper frames as \"benchmark-dependent contamination effects\" — ContamCal calibrates in both directions.\n86\t3. **GSM8K** — mathematical reasoning free-form (~1.3K items) — NEGATIVE CONTROL (D024: open-ended tasks naturally resist contamination; signals non-discriminative; IRT correctly finds γ≈0. Paper frames as \"ContamCal identifies benchmarks not requiring calibration\")\n87\t4. **HumanEval** (appendix only) — code generation (N=164). Demoted D009: paraphrase QC 16%.\n88\t\n89\t## Baselines (7)\n90\t0. **Raw (no adjustment)** — report original benchmark scores as anchor\n91\t1. **Remove-and-rescore** — drop flagged items, rescore on remaining\n92\t2. **Single-signal 2PL+γ** — best single detection signal + simpler IRT\n93\t3. **Vanilla 4PL** — standard IRT without contamination covariate (γ=0)\n94\t4. **Linear exposure regression + bootstrap CI** — simplest post-hoc adjustment (tests if IRT complexity is necessary)\n95\t5. **Emperor's mitigation strategies** — rephrasing + option shuffling (2 representative strategies from ICML 2025)\n96\t6. **DCR fuzzy inference (EMNLP 2025)** — the only existing method producing adjusted accuracy; direct predecessor, must compare\n97\t\n98\t## Local Independence Discussion\n99\tStandard IRT assumes independent examinees. In LLM evaluation, a single model produces all item responses — responses are correlated through shared model parameters. This fundamentally alters local independence. Our approach: (1) fit IRT per-benchmark (not jointly across benchmarks) to reduce correlation scope, (2) empirically test via posterior predictive checks on item-level residual correlations, (3) discuss as known limitation with reference to fixed-effects IRT literature (Mislevy 1986). The coverage gap (if observed) may partly reflect this violation — measurement error model for exposure partially compensates by widening CIs.\n100\t\n101\t## Required Figures (Dream D2: \"zero figures\" flagged as serious deficiency)\n102\t1. Pipeline overview diagram (2-stage: RRF → 4PL+γ IRT)\n103\t2. Signal × scale × dosage AUC heatmap (exp-008) — 'regime' axis replaced by scale+dosage per D022\n104\t3. Dose-response curves (contamination dosage vs accuracy inflation, exp-001/006)\n105\t4. Identifiability phase diagram (AUC × N_models → γ recoverability, exp-004)\n106\t5. Calibrated-vs-true accuracy scatter with CIs (exp-006)\n107\t\n108\t## Citation Requirements (35+ minimum, expanded per Dream D2)\n109\tMust include: Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017, De Boeck & Wilson 2004, Adams et al. 1997, San Martín et al. 2009, Maris & Bechger 2009, Cormack et al. 2009 (RRF), Mislevy 1986, Lalor et al. AAAI 2024, LiveBench, TinyBenchmarks (Polo et al. 2024), KDS, CDD, contamination surveys, SBC (Talts et al. 2018), HiBayES, plus all signals/methods cited in IDEA_BRIEF.\n110\tDream D2 additions (all 3 tiers flagged):\n111\t- DIF literature: Holland & Wainer 1993, Thissen et al. 1993, Zumbo 1999 (γ_k is essentially DIF between exposure groups)\n112\t- ML calibration: Guo et al. 2017, Platt 1999 (disambiguate \"calibration\" — ours is decontamination, not probability calibration)\n113\t- Conformal prediction: Angelopoulos & Bates 2023 (alternative UQ with distribution-free coverage guarantees; discuss why Bayesian IRT preferred)\n114\t- Data attribution: Pruthi et al. 2020 (TracIn), Park et al. 2023 (TRAK) — potential additional signals, mention in Future Work\n115\t- Multidimensional IRT: Reckase 2009 (acknowledge unidimensional θ for MMLU as simplification in Limitations)\n116\tD025 additions (all 3 Dream tiers flagged):\n117\t- MIA foundations: Shokri et al. 2017 (membership inference attacks), Carlini et al. 2022 (extracting training data) — contextualize Min-K++ reversal in MIA literature\n118\t- Alternative evaluation: HELM (Liang et al. 2023), Chatbot Arena (Zheng et al. 2023) — discuss in Related Work as contamination-bypassing alternatives\n119\t- Deeper DCR engagement: Oren et al. 2024 structural comparison\n120\t- Burnell et al. 2023: multidimensional IRT for LLMs (relevant to unidimensional θ limitation)\n121\t\n122\t## Discussion Notes\n123\t\n124\t### D045: 0.5B Position Bias and MC Benchmark Exclusion (2026-05-15)\n125\t- **Finding**: Qwen2.5-0.5B exhibits severe position bias in logprob-based MC evaluation. MMLU prediction distribution: d000 A=46% → d100 A=98%. SFT contamination amplifies pre-existing position prior rather than encoding knowledge.\n126\t- **Decision**: Exclude 0.5B MMLU/ARC data from IRT pooling. Retain 0.5B GSM8K/HumanEval (free-form, unaffected). 1.5B/3B all benchmarks normal.\n127\t- **Paper treatment**: Report in Discussion as \"capacity threshold for reliable MC evaluation.\" The 0.5B dosage-accuracy decline (40→23% MMLU) is position bias amplification, not knowledge degradation. This reinforces Concern #22 (SFT capacity artifact) and motivates the 1.5B+ focus in main results.\n128\t- **Broader implication**: Logprob-based MC evaluation at small scales may be unreliable across model families — note as limitation and suggest token-level or generation-based alternatives for sub-1B models.\n129\t\n130\t## Fisher Information Derivation (Dream D2: all 3 tiers flagged as critical gap)\n131\tMust derive I(γ) for 4PL+γ model in appendix. Currently stated without proof.\n132\tThe derivation must show: (1) relationship between signal AUC and γ recoverability, (2) approximation assumptions, (3) how measurement error factors in.\n133\tThis is CPU-only theoretical work — can be done in parallel with GPU experiments.\n134\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"9fcda0b6-5767-4cb0-acd5-850122790056","uuid":"c8eaae08-61f0-433a-a5b2-61de9289aa74","timestamp":"2026-05-16T11:11:09.325Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0509_13/projects/contamcal-irt-calibration/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260516T104822/best/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — contamcal-irt-calibration\n\n## Target Venue\n- **Primary: EMNLP 2026 ARR May 25** (D006: reinstated per user directive, 12 days)\n- Fallback: AAAI 2027 (Jul-Aug 2026) / ICLR 2027 (Sep 2026)\n- Track: Machine Learning for NLP / Resources and Evaluation\n- Aligns with EMNLP 2026 special theme \"New Missions for NLP Research\" (explicitly invites contamination/data governance work)\n\n## Core Insight (D039 pivot + D108 domain heterogeneity)\nBenchmark contamination effects are far more complex than \"scores go up.\" Through controlled SFT injection and contamination-augmented IRT analysis, we reveal: effects exhibit domain-level heterogeneity within benchmarks (MMLU: Simpson's paradox — aggregate γ≈0 masks knowledge↑ vs reasoning↓ pattern), are benchmark-dependent across benchmarks (ARC: forgetting vs GSM8K: inflation), scale-dependent (catastrophic at 0.5B, negligible at 7B), and detection signals exhibit fundamental failure modes (Min-K++ directional reversal). ContamCal uses IRT with per-benchmark γ_k to systematically characterize these behaviors and establish identifiability boundaries. Calibration is possible conditionally — when model complexity matches benchmark structure (2PL for open-ended, 4PL for MCQ) and signal quality is sufficient (recovery validated down to AUC=0.55, lowest tested, at J≥50) — but the primary contribution is the characterization framework itself. Grounded in educational measurement's item compromise IRT (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) and explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004).\n\n## Core Claims (4 条 — D039 reorder: characterization first, calibration demoted)\n\n1. **Characterization Claim (PRIMARY — D108: domain heterogeneity evidence)**: Contamination effects exhibit domain-level heterogeneity within benchmarks, are benchmark-dependent across benchmarks, and scale-dependent. Within MMLU, stratification by cognitive domain (57 subjects → 8 domains) reveals Simpson's paradox: aggregate γ≈0 masks domain-level heterogeneity (5 domains significant, γ range: -0.55 to +1.34). Pattern: knowledge-intensive domains (history, law) show positive γ; reasoning-intensive domains (STEM, formal logic) show negative γ. Claim is based on **pattern/ranking consistency**, not absolute γ values (PPP=0 in 4/5 domains, see strategy below). Pending N=24 rerun confirmation before upgrading to \"domain-dependent characterization.\" Across benchmarks: ARC forgetting (γ < 0, CI excludes zero, 3 independent evidence chains) vs GSM8K inflation (γ > 0). Effects are scale-modulated: catastrophic at 0.5B (-17pp ARC), mild at 3B, negligible at 7B. This challenges the dominant \"contamination = uniform inflation\" narrative. Cross-family consistency with Llama-3.2-1B suggests contamination susceptibility is a benchmark × domain property, not a model family artifact.\n   **PPP presentation strategy (Reviewer warning)**: Domain-level PPP=0 reflects known sensitivity of posterior predictive checks in large-N IRT (Levy & Svetina 2011; Sinharay 2006). Paper claims based on **pattern/ranking** (knowledge > reasoning domains) and **heterogeneity** (Simpson's paradox), NOT absolute γ values. Will rerun with N=24 (adding 3B) — if γ attenuates, interpret as scale-dependent contamination sensitivity (valuable finding, narrative adjustment needed). Consider limited-information fit indices (M2*, RMSEA2) as robustness check.\n\n2. **Signal Failure Claim (CO-PRIMARY, headline finding per D039 dream 三档一致)**: Min-K++ exhibits directional reversal at small model scales (Cohen's d = -1.2 to -2.7 at 0.5B d100) — actively misleading, not merely ineffective. S1 embedding similarity is the only reliable signal (AUC>0.95). Multi-signal RRF degrades to S1-only in practice — paper is transparent about this (D039: all 3 dream tiers require honesty here). Training-free RRF remains the principled aggregation design (meta-circularity argument). Must distinguish item-level detection AUC from model-level Cohen's d.\n   **C2 contingency**: \"Scale-dependent\" unverified beyond 0.5B. If 1.5B/3B S2/S4 remain ineffective → downgrade to \"method-specific signal effectiveness.\" Min-K++ reversal remains novel regardless.\n\n3. **Identifiability Claim**: Fisher Information analysis + simulation sweep (20 conditions, 200 runs). Recovery succeeds down to AUC=0.55 (lowest tested) at N≥50; the lower bound remains open. LLM evaluation inherently operates below N≥50 (typically N=8-30), creating a structural domain-method mismatch — this is the real \"impossibility,\" not merely \"IRT needs more data.\" CI width ∝ N^{-0.48} (simulation). Coverage collapse at N<30 confirmed empirically (D036). D039 dream worst-tier caution: frame as domain mismatch insight, not as surprising impossibility.\n\n4. **Conditional Calibration Claim (DEMOTED from old Claim 1 per D039)**: When IRT model complexity matches benchmark structure (2PL for open-ended, 4PL for MCQ) AND signal quality sufficient (validated down to AUC=0.55 at J≥50), calibration produces usable point estimates (ARC MAE=0.035). Coverage remains below nominal (20-69% at 95%) — present calibrated CIs as directional indicators, not precise credible intervals. Calibration gating criterion (D039 dream recommendation): γ significant AND PPP ∈ [0.1, 0.9] AND calibrated MAE < raw MAE. GSM8K overcorrection (MAE 11× worse than raw) is an honest negative result demonstrating the gating criterion's necessity.\n\n## Contributions (Hierarchical — D039 rebalanced per Dream 2nd round)\n\n### Core (characterization-led; calibration conditional)\n1. **Domain-dependent contamination characterization** via IRT γ_k: bidirectional effects at domain level (Simpson's paradox within MMLU), scale modulation, three-regime taxonomy (forgetting / selective memorization / near-immunity). γ heterogeneity driven by cognitive task type (knowledge vs reasoning domains)\n2. **Signal failure characterization**: Min-K++ directional reversal (Cohen's d = -1.2 to -2.7) — paper's most citation-worthy empirical finding (D025 + D039 dream 三档一致). Systematic signal × scale × dosage AUC mapping. Honest reporting that RRF = S1-only in practice\n3. **Fisher Information identifiability analysis**: structural vs practical thresholds with domain-method mismatch insight. Strongest theoretical contribution (D025 + D039 一致)\n\n### Supporting\n4. ContamCal framework: two-stage pipeline (S1 provenance → contamination-augmented IRT with per-benchmark γ_k). Conditional calibration with gating criterion. ME model as ablation\n5. Dual-track validation + 7 baselines + 6 metrics (incl. SBC). Controlled SFT injection (internal) + ecological RL (external)\n\n## Story Spine\n- **Problem**: Benchmark scores are increasingly unreliable due to training data contamination. Rephrased test items inflate MMLU accuracy by 8-15 points while evading n-gram decontamination. RL post-training may further complicate detection (under investigation, exp-009). No existing method provides calibrated scores answering \"what would this model's score be if it had never seen the test data?\"\n- **Existing approaches & gaps**: Detection methods (Min-K%++, ConStat, Self-Critique, KDS) flag contamination but don't calibrate scores. IRT methods (PSN-IRT, Growing Pains, ATLAS, Lalor et al. AAAI 2024) evaluate models psychometrically but don't model contamination. DCR provides adjusted accuracy but lacks uncertainty bounds and RL handling. HiBayES provides Bayesian uncertainty but ignores contamination. Emperor's New Clothes (ICML 2025) demonstrates that all 20 mitigation strategies fail — motivating post-hoc calibration. Dynamic benchmarks (LiveBench) offer an alternative but require continuous curation.\n- **Our framing (D039 pivot)**: ContamCal is a diagnostic framework that uses IRT to systematically characterize how contamination affects benchmark scores. Rather than claiming to \"fix\" contaminated scores, we reveal the structure of contamination effects — they are bidirectional, scale-dependent, and benchmark-specific — and establish the conditions under which statistical calibration is and isn't feasible. Grounded in educational measurement's item compromise IRT (Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017) and explanatory IRT (De Boeck & Wilson 2004), with 3 structural adaptations: (a) independent examinees → single model with joint item distribution, (b) binary leakage → continuous exposure scores, (c) stable ability → regime-conditional ability.\n- **Headline result template (D039 — characterization-led)**: \"Contamination effects are benchmark-dependent: IRT γ captures forgetting on ARC (γ={X}, CI excludes zero) and inflation on GSM8K (γ={Y}), with scale modulation from catastrophic (-{Z}pp at 0.5B) to negligible at 7B. Detection signal Min-K++ actively reverses direction at small scales (Cohen's d = {W}), making single-signal contamination detection unreliable. Fisher Information analysis establishes that reliable γ recovery requires N≥50 models; recovery succeeded down to AUC=0.55 (lowest tested), with the lower bound remaining open — conditions rarely met in current LLM evaluation.\"\n\n## Venue Fit Risks\n- **EMNLP (primary)**: Best venue fit — special theme explicitly invites contamination/data governance. Reviewers deeply familiar with NLP contamination literature (advantage: no need to motivate the problem heavily). Risk: reviewers may be less familiar with IRT/psychometric methods (mitigation: add intuitive explanations, visual pipeline overview, and connection to familiar NLP concepts like calibration/confidence estimation). Risk: may expect broader model coverage beyond Qwen2.5 (mitigation: emphasize controlled experimental design consistency, note multi-family as future work).\n- **AAAI/ICLR (fallback)**: Strong fit for Bayesian/probabilistic methods. Reviewers may need more contamination context but appreciate methodological rigor.\n- **Novelty positioning**: Core method is cross-disciplinary synthesis (detection signals + explanatory IRT + measurement error). NOT positioned as \"new algorithm\" — positioned as (a) signal effectiveness characterization as empirically novel finding, (b) identifiability bounds with theoretical grounding, (c) first framework bridging detection → calibration with proper UQ. RRF is a principled design choice (meta-circularity argument), not claimed as methodological contribution.\n- **RL limitation**: RL models get wide CIs. This is honest — frame as \"calibration is only as good as its inputs.\" Expected regret quantification uses held-out clean benchmark as ground truth (NOT calibrated scores, to avoid circularity).\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns & Defenses\n\n| # | Concern | Defense | Experiment |\n|---|---------|---------|------------|\n| 1 | θ and γ identifiable? | Fisher Information analysis + 20-condition simulation sweep (200 runs) + MVP validation | exp-004 |\n| 2 | Why not learned aggregation? | Meta-circularity: learned aggregator's training data may itself be contaminated; RRF is design choice, not contribution | exp-003 |\n| 3 | SFT injection ≠ real contamination | Dual-track: controlled SFT (internal) + ecological RL (external) + near-zero γ check (specificity). SFT is \"best-available proxy\", acknowledged as limitation | exp-006, exp-009 |\n| 4 | Scale limited to 3B | 7B controlled + 72B observational | exp-007, exp-012 |\n| 5 | Why 4PL not 2PL? | MVP: 2PL γ=0.16 vs 4PL γ=0.34 at N=100; c/d necessary for realistic item modeling | exp-006 |\n| 6 | Too few models (N≈15)? | D025 multi-seed strategy: 3 scales × 4+ dosages × 2-3 seeds = 24-36 models pooled. Target N≥30 minimum (N≥50 ideal). Simulation sweep (exp-004) establishes identifiability at actual N. Growing Pains pre-estimation as backup at very low N | exp-004, exp-001 |\n| 7 | Noisy exposure scores | Measurement error model (exposure as noisy observation of latent τ) propagates uncertainty into posteriors automatically | exp-005 |\n| 8 | No baselines | 7 baselines including DCR (EMNLP 2025) + bootstrap CIs on all comparisons | exp-006 |\n| 9 | Single global γ misspecified? | Per-benchmark γ_k is primary (natural in per-benchmark fitting); joint model with shared global γ is ablation. Report both to show contamination effect heterogeneity across benchmarks | exp-006 |\n| 10 | Local independence violated? | Discussed explicitly; single model responds to all items → item responses correlated through shared parameters. Empirically test via posterior predictive checks on item-level residual correlations. This is a known IRT limitation when N_examinees is small. | exp-006 |\n| 11 | Single model family? | Acknowledged as limitation. Qwen2.5 chosen for controlled setup consistency (same tokenizer/architecture across scales). Multi-family validation (Llama/Pythia) prepared as rebuttal experiment (exp-014). | exp-014 (rebuttal) |\n| 12 | RL regret circular? | Ground truth for regret = held-out clean benchmark scores, NOT calibrated scores. Explicitly stated | exp-009 |\n| 13 | S2/S4 fail at 0.5B — RRF is just S1? | Scale-dependent: honestly report single-signal regime at 0.5B. Verify S2/S4 at 1.5B/3B. RRF value = principled aggregation when signals ARE available + graceful degradation when not. | exp-008, exp-002 |\n| 14 | d100 AUC=0.5 as signal failure? | Labeling artifact: d100 contaminates ALL items → labels lose discriminative power. Must NOT be interpreted as signal degradation. Present only d005/d025 for signal evaluation. | exp-008 |\n| 15 | Novelty ceiling — standard IRT synthesis? (D025, all 3 Dream tiers) | Positioned as cross-disciplinary synthesis, NOT new algorithm. Emergent value: (a) per-benchmark γ_k heterogeneity as benchmark property, (b) Min-K++ reversal as cautionary finding, (c) Fisher Info identifiability boundaries. If 4PL vs 2PL gap small → acknowledge honestly but argue c/d params needed for realistic MC modeling. Report oracle-γ ablation to decompose MAE. | exp-006 |\n| 16 | Per-benchmark γ_k via separate fits, not hierarchical? (D025, [M][W]) | Per-benchmark separate fits = primary (natural, avoids cross-benchmark correlation). Hierarchical γ_k ~ Normal(μ_γ, σ_γ) as ablation to formally test heterogeneity and share info across benchmarks. Both reported. | exp-006 |\n| 17 | Coverage gap — 85% vs 95% nominal? (D025, all 3 tiers) | SBC diagnostic to identify source. Possible causes: local independence violation, insufficient N, model misspecification. Report SBC alongside posterior predictive checks. If gap is from local independence → discuss as known IRT limitation with references. | exp-004, exp-006 |\n| 18 | Why not just S1 alone? (D025, [M][W]) | S1-only is a baseline (#2 single-signal 2PL+γ). If RRF ≤ S1-only → present as \"S1+IRT calibration with extensible multi-signal architecture for future signals.\" Meta-circularity argument for training-free aggregation still holds. | exp-003 |\n| 19 | Coverage collapse 20-33% even with oracle? (D036) | Far worse than #17 anticipated. PPP extremes (ARC=1.0, GSM8K=0.0) = systematic misspecification. Path: (a) increase N via multi-seed pooling, (b) check exp-004 sweep coverage at matching N, (c) if additive γ insufficient → item-level heterogeneity or hierarchical structure. Fallback: lead with MAE+ranking, present coverage honestly. | exp-006, exp-004 |\n| 20 | GSM8K overcorrection — calibration 11× worse than raw (D039, all 3 dream tiers) | Honest negative result. Implement calibration gating: γ significant AND PPP ∈ [0.1,0.9] AND calibrated MAE < raw MAE. Report raw scores when gating fails. Reframe: ContamCal identifies when NOT to calibrate. | exp-006 |\n| 21 | Linear regression nearly as good as IRT (D039, all 3 tiers) | B4 MAE=0.040 vs ContamCal MAE=0.035 on ARC. IRT's value is NOT better MAE — it's interpretable per-item parameters, benchmark-dependent γ_k characterization, and theoretical grounding. Bootstrap CI on MAE gap to show significance (or acknowledge non-significance). | exp-006 |\n| 22 | SFT capacity artifact ≠ contamination (D039, all 3 tiers) | 0.5B ARC forgetting (-17pp) may be capacity displacement, not contamination-specific. Need: (a) SFT-on-random-data control at 0.5B to isolate capacity vs contamination, (b) focus on 1.5B/3B where capacity effects are minimal (<2pp). Acknowledge 0.5B forgetting as combined SFT+contamination effect. | exp-001 |\n| 23 | N=8/16 contradicts paper's own N≥50 recommendation (D039, all 3 tiers) | Internal contradiction. Path: (a) add N=8 condition to simulation sweep for calibrated expectations, (b) increase N via multi-seed (target N≥30), (c) if N<30 → explicitly position as preliminary with caveats. SBC diagnostic at actual N to decompose coverage gap. | exp-004, exp-006 |\n\n## Evaluation Protocol (6 Metrics — D025 added SBC)\n1. **Calibration error**: MAE between calibrated score and ground-truth clean accuracy, with bootstrap 95% CIs\n2. **Interval coverage**: Fraction of true values within 50%/80%/95% credible intervals\n3. **Interval sharpness**: Average CI width (tighter is better at fixed coverage)\n4. **Ranking preservation**: Kendall's τ between calibrated and ground-truth model rankings\n5. **Dose-response monotonicity**: γ effect increases monotonically with contamination dosage\n6. **SBC diagnostic** (D025): Simulation-Based Calibration (Talts et al. 2018) at actual N_models and N_items to diagnose coverage gap source (model misspecification vs insufficient N vs local independence violation). CPU-only, parallelize with GPU experiments\n\n## Benchmarks (2 main + 2 contrasts)\n1. **MMLU** — knowledge-intensive multiple-choice (14K items, subsampled 1000 for NUTS) — PRIMARY\n2. **ARC-Challenge** — science reasoning multiple-choice (~1.2K items) — PRIMARY. ⚠️ D036: gamma consistently negative (forgetting, not inflation). 3 independent evidence chains: s42 raw (-17.5pp), s123 raw (-10.2pp), oracle IRT (γ=-2.83). Scale-dependent: 3B d100 ARC=84% (mild forgetting) vs 0.5B d100 ARC=29-37% (catastrophic). Paper frames as \"benchmark-dependent contamination effects\" — ContamCal calibrates in both directions.\n3. **GSM8K** — mathematical reasoning free-form (~1.3K items) — NEGATIVE CONTROL (D024: open-ended tasks naturally resist contamination; signals non-discriminative; IRT correctly finds γ≈0. Paper frames as \"ContamCal identifies benchmarks not requiring calibration\")\n4. **HumanEval** (appendix only) — code generation (N=164). Demoted D009: paraphrase QC 16%.\n\n## Baselines (7)\n0. **Raw (no adjustment)** — report original benchmark scores as anchor\n1. **Remove-and-rescore** — drop flagged items, rescore on remaining\n2. **Single-signal 2PL+γ** — best single detection signal + simpler IRT\n3. **Vanilla 4PL** — standard IRT without contamination covariate (γ=0)\n4. **Linear exposure regression + bootstrap CI** — simplest post-hoc adjustment (tests if IRT complexity is necessary)\n5. **Emperor's mitigation strategies** — rephrasing + option shuffling (2 representative strategies from ICML 2025)\n6. **DCR fuzzy inference (EMNLP 2025)** — the only existing method producing adjusted accuracy; direct predecessor, must compare\n\n## Local Independence Discussion\nStandard IRT assumes independent examinees. In LLM evaluation, a single model produces all item responses — responses are correlated through shared model parameters. This fundamentally alters local independence. Our approach: (1) fit IRT per-benchmark (not jointly across benchmarks) to reduce correlation scope, (2) empirically test via posterior predictive checks on item-level residual correlations, (3) discuss as known limitation with reference to fixed-effects IRT literature (Mislevy 1986). The coverage gap (if observed) may partly reflect this violation — measurement error model for exposure partially compensates by widening CIs.\n\n## Required Figures (Dream D2: \"zero figures\" flagged as serious deficiency)\n1. Pipeline overview diagram (2-stage: RRF → 4PL+γ IRT)\n2. Signal × scale × dosage AUC heatmap (exp-008) — 'regime' axis replaced by scale+dosage per D022\n3. Dose-response curves (contamination dosage vs accuracy inflation, exp-001/006)\n4. Identifiability phase diagram (AUC × N_models → γ recoverability, exp-004)\n5. Calibrated-vs-true accuracy scatter with CIs (exp-006)\n\n## Citation Requirements (35+ minimum, expanded per Dream D2)\nMust include: Eckerly 2017, Sinharay 2017, De Boeck & Wilson 2004, Adams et al. 1997, San Martín et al. 2009, Maris & Bechger 2009, Cormack et al. 2009 (RRF), Mislevy 1986, Lalor et al. AAAI 2024, LiveBench, TinyBenchmarks (Polo et al. 2024), KDS, CDD, contamination surveys, SBC (Talts et al. 2018), HiBayES, plus all signals/methods cited in IDEA_BRIEF.\nDream D2 additions (all 3 tiers flagged):\n- DIF literature: Holland & Wainer 1993, Thissen et al. 1993, Zumbo 1999 (γ_k is essentially DIF between exposure groups)\n- ML calibration: Guo et al. 2017, Platt 1999 (disambiguate \"calibration\" — ours is decontamination, not probability calibration)\n- Conformal prediction: Angelopoulos & Bates 2023 (alternative UQ with distribution-free coverage guarantees; discuss why Bayesian IRT preferred)\n- Data attribution: Pruthi et al. 2020 (TracIn), Park et al. 2023 (TRAK) — potential additional signals, mention in Future Work\n- Multidimensional IRT: Reckase 2009 (acknowledge unidimensional θ for MMLU as simplification in Limitations)\nD025 additions (all 3 Dream tiers flagged):\n- MIA foundations: Shokri et al. 2017 (membership inference attacks), Carlini et al. 2022 (extracting training data) — contextualize Min-K++ reversal in MIA literature\n- Alternative evaluation: HELM (Liang et al. 2023), Chatbot Arena (Zheng et al. 2023) — discuss in Related Work as contamination-bypassing alternatives\n- Deeper DCR engagement: Oren et al. 2024 structural comparison\n- Burnell et al. 2023: multidimensional IRT for LLMs (relevant to unidimensional θ limitation)\n\n## Discussion Notes\n\n### D045: 0.5B Position Bias and MC Benchmark Exclusion (2026-05-15)\n- **Finding**: Qwen2.5-0.5B exhibits severe position bias in logprob-based MC evaluation. MMLU prediction distribution: d000 A=46% → d100 A=98%. SFT contamination amplifies pre-existing position prior rather than encoding knowledge.\n- **Decision**: Exclude 0.5B MMLU/ARC data from IRT pooling. Retain 0.5B GSM8K/HumanEval (free-form, unaffected). 1.5B/3B all benchmarks normal.\n- **Paper treatment**: Report in Discussion as \"capacity threshold for reliable MC evaluation.\" The 0.5B dosage-accuracy decline (40→23% MMLU) is position bias amplification, not knowledge degradation. This reinforces Concern #22 (SFT capacity artifact) and motivates the 1.5B+ focus in main results.\n- **Broader implication**: Logprob-based MC evaluation at small scales may be unreliable across model families — note as limitation and suggest token-level or generation-based alternatives for sub-1B models.\n\n## Fisher Information Derivation (Dream D2: all 3 tiers flagged as critical gap)\nMust derive I(γ) for 4PL+γ model in appendix. Currently stated without proof.\nThe derivation must show: (1) relationship between signal AUC and γ recoverability, (2) approximation assumptions, (3) how measurement error factors in.\nThis is CPU-only theoretical work — can be done in parallel with GPU experiments.\n","numLines":134,"startLine":1,"totalLines":134}}}
